eight days after Hurricane Maria  flattened much of Puerto Rico basic  resources like food water fuel and  medicine are still hard to find supplies  have arrived but they're not getting  delivered to the 3.4 million American  citizens on the island the lack of power  and communication is hindering relief  efforts officials say more than 91  percent of Puerto Rico's cellphone sites  are out of service David Berg no is  outside a gas station in San Juan where  people are desperate for fuel David good  morning it's been a week since the  hurricane made landfall and these are  Americans sitting in line sleeping in  their car desperately trying to get fuel  that lady was here when they ran out she  was almost at the front of the line this  family right here slept in their car and  they've been waiting since 9:00 a.m.  yesterday this is the gas station in San  Juan where everybody's flocking to they  ran out of fuel here about 6 o'clock  local time last night and they're going  to get another shipment today but  they're running out every day officials  have told us the local Children's  Hospital here in San Juan is going to  run out of diesel if they don't get more  by new again this morning more than 3000  shipping containers are sitting at the  port of San Juan in these containers we  have medicines there's water Jose Ayala  is vice president of Crowley shipping  services Puerto Rico  he says the stalled supplies could help  more than a half million Puerto Ricans  it's maddening to look out here and see  it sitting knowing that there are people  desperate for food and water  yeah it's that and it's frustrating as  well unless trucking companies start  showing up unfortunately there is  nothing that we can do the governor  Ricardo Rose AO told us there's a  shortage of truck drivers to deliver the  essentials the bus drivers that would  traditionally take these foods they  don't they're not a pyramid you can't  find them you can't reach them you can't  find out more reach them most roads are  still blocked by debris and fuel is in  very short supply lines still stretch  for a 1/2 mile or more as people wait in  the stifling heat for basic necessities  gas to power generators this is our  third day getting gas it's  with the tanks they just hold so very  little just one gallon two gallon five  gallons it's just not enough it's very  bad it is horrible and we are we're  American citizens we're not better than  anyone but we're not worse than anyone  David beg no in San Juan
 

